VERSUS

Arvind Singhania
S/o Late Shri B. K. Singhania,
R/o 12, Golf Links, New Delhi
...Accused / Applicant

FIR No.: 112/2025, P.S. Barakhamba Road
U/Sections: 409, 420, 120B IPC / Sections 316, 318 BNS + Section 447 Companies Act, 2013

APPLICATION UNDER SECTION 250 BNSS, 2023 (SECTION 227 CrPC) FOR DISCHARGE

MOST RESPECTFULLY SHOWETH:

1. That the Applicant has been maliciously prosecuted in respect of transactions that were fully disclosed in statutory filings, approved by the Board of Directors, and audited by independent statutory auditors.

2. That the foundational ingredient of Section 420 IPC / Section 318 BNS—namely fraudulent inducement with dishonest intention at inception—is completely absent. All payments were made under legitimate supply contracts supported by e-way bills and GST returns.

3. That Section 409 IPC is legally untenable against a company director acting under board authority, as settled by the Hon'ble Supreme Court in Sunil Bharti Mittal v. CBI (2015) 4 SCC 609, holding that vicarious liability cannot be imputed in the absence of explicit statutory provision and active personal complicity.

4. That the Complainant has already instituted parallel proceedings under Sections 241/242 of the Companies Act, 2013 before the National Company Law Tribunal (NCLT), New Delhi on identical allegations. A civil dispute of corporate governance cannot be given a criminal cloak.

5. That continuing the criminal trial would amount to an abuse of the process of the court when no prima facie case is made out on the documents submitted by the prosecution itself.

PRAYER:
Wherefore, it is respectfully prayed that this Hon'ble Court may graciously be pleased to discharge the Applicant of all charges framed under Sections 409, 420, 120B IPC and Section 447 Companies Act.

Applicant / Counsel for Applicant
New Delhi, Dated: 20.04.2025
